S = [{'id': 'architect', 'cat': 'build', 'tag': 'Designs the shape before the code. Sketches types and signatures with empty bodies, gets competing sketches from several models, then fills in code against the winner.', 'invoke': '/architect the sync engine needs offline edits. sketch the types and module boundaries before writing anything.', 'how': ['Phase A grounds the problem by running <code>how</code> over every system the new code touches, and <code>why</code> too when ownership or layering changes. Only true greenfield work skips it.', 'Phase B hands the sketch task to <code>arena</code>, using the <code>architect runners</code> line from <code>pstack-models.mdc</code> (defaults <code>claude-opus-5-5-xhigh</code> and <code>grok-4.7-xhigh-fast</code>). At least two structurally distinct candidates are required.', 'Every candidate is screened against <code>references/design-red-flags.md</code> and compared on interface depth: the smaller public surface that hides more wins.', 'Phase C, a human checkpoint, is opt-in only. By default the synthesized sketch goes straight to implementation and can ship as its own scaffold commit.', 'Phase D fills the <code>not implemented</code> bodies. The sketch is the contract, so any deviation gets surfaced rather than absorbed.', 'Phase E scraps the sketch when friction forms a pattern (repeated workarounds, escape-hatch types, a sudden need for a lock), then re-grounds and re-runs arena with a smaller design.'], 'rules': ['Design it twice. Require at least two structurally distinct candidates before synthesis, even when the first looks sufficient.', 'Assume the next contributor is an agent that sees only the files it opened, copies the nearest example, and takes the shortest path that compiles.', 'Deviations from it are signal to surface, not friction to absorb silently.', 'Complexity in the data is not complexity in the design.'], 'lesson': 'It reuses <code>arena</code> for the multi-model part instead of duplicating it, and only adds the design-specific rules on top: two distinct shapes, a red-flag screen, and an explicit trigger list for scrapping a design.'}, {'id': 'arena', 'cat': 'build', 'tag': 'A bake-off. N subagents attempt the same artifact, a judge from another model family scores them, and the best one becomes the base with the strongest ideas from the rest grafted in.', 'invoke': '/arena write the retry policy for the upload client. i want to see a few shapes before we pick one.', 'how': ['Frame: one shared prompt is the contract. The picker writes a 3–6 item rubric that candidates never see, and gives each candidate its own worktree or <code>/tmp/arena-&lt;slug&gt;/candidate-&lt;n&gt;/</code>.', 'Fan out: all runners spawn in one message in the background, from the <code>arena runners</code> line. If the Task tool rejects a model, that seat runs on its family default or the closest valid slug; if seats drop below two, runners are added back. Each returns the artifact plus a note on what it rejected.', "Cross-judge: once every candidate is done, one readonly judge scores each criterion and recommends a base. Since 0.15.36 the judge is never from the parent's family; with no such model, Phase C skips the judge and the note says so.", 'Pick: the parent reads every candidate in full, scores criterion by criterion, and compares with the judge. Ties go to the cleaner boundary or smaller API.', 'Graft: one or two ideas per loser are folded in by hand. Convergence means ship the consensus. Wild divergence means the framing was wrong, so re-run.', 'Verify the result like any output, and send a missed problem back to the framing or graft phase.'], 'rules': ['Every candidate gets the same prompt, so the prompt is the contract.', 'Pick the base a future maintainer can extend most easily without breaking invariants.', 'If they wildly diverge, Phase A was under-specified. Reframe and re-run rather than averaging the divergence.'], 'lesson': 'The rubric is hidden from candidates and the judge comes from a different model family. Those two choices are what make a multi-model comparison worth more than one long attempt.'}, {'id': 'bro', 'cat': 'write', 'tag': 'Asks the agent to say its last message again in plain language.', 'invoke': '/bro', 'how': ['The whole body is three sentences: restate the last message, drop the jargon, say it simply like one person to another.', 'The frontmatter sets <code>disable-model-invocation: true</code>, so it runs only when typed. The folder holds nothing but SKILL.md.', 'The guide’s recipes chapter presents it as the entire prompt: <code>/bro</code>, used when a reply is thorough but still unclear.', '<code>/ketchup</code> borrows it: “Use <code>/bro</code>’s voice, not its task”, so catch-up reports inherit the plain register.'], 'rules': ['Stop using jargon and speak coherently.', 'State it more simply and concisely, like one human talking to another.'], 'lesson': 'A skill can be a saved prompt. When the instruction fits in one line, the slash command is the whole value: you stop retyping it.'}, {'id': 'correct', 'cat': 'learn', 'tag': 'Turns the mistakes agents keep repeating in a repo into things the repo itself prevents, preferring architecture, then types and lints, then tests, and docs last.', 'invoke': '/correct agents keep importing the db client directly instead of going through the repository layer. make that impossible.', 'how': ['Reads recent commits, reverts, review comments, agent instruction files and workaround comments, then groups mistakes into classes. A class counts once it has happened twice.', 'Fixes each class at the highest level that works: architecture first (one owner per state, hidden internals, one source of truth).', 'Then types, or a lint or CI check whose error message names the thing to use instead. A pattern that is already common fails only on new additions.', 'Then tests that actually fail when behavior breaks. Docs and agent rules come last, only for judgment calls.', "Each check must be shown to fail on a real past mistake, and runs the same locally and in CI. Exceptions need a reason, an expiry date and a human's approval.", 'Keeps a rule table in the agent instruction file pairing every rule with what enforces it.'], 'rules': ['Design the repo so a change that looks right from one file is right for the whole repo.', 'Write docs or agent rules last, only for judgment calls.', 'Nothing fails when an agent skips them.'], 'lesson': 'This is the encode-lessons-in-structure principle turned into a procedure, with an explicit ladder of enforcement levels and a proof requirement on each rung.'}, {'id': 'in-your-own-words', 'cat': 'understand', 'tag': "Asks the agent to restate the user's goals and the problem behind them, before it does anything.", 'invoke': '/in-your-own-words', 'how': ['The body is one sentence: restate the user’s goals and the problem they are trying to solve.', 'It runs alone, with no playbook. The guide’s recipes chapter: “<code>/in-your-own-words</code> asks for the restatement alone, with no playbook…”', 'The README notes it “runs only when you type <code>/in-your-own-words</code>”, matching its <code>disable-model-invocation: true</code> frontmatter.', '<code>/poteto-help</code> lists it for “Check that the agent understood their goal and the problem before it starts”.'], 'rules': ["Restate in your own words what you think the user's goals are and what problem they're trying to solve.", 'Use for /in-your-own-words.'], 'lesson': 'Like <code>/bro</code>, a one-line prompt saved as a command. It catches a misread goal before any work is spent on it.'}, {'id': 'sharpen', 'cat': 'write', 'tag': 'Rewrites a prompt so the task goes deeper rather than wider, by asking what a top professional would do differently.', 'invoke': '/sharpen write a blog post about our new caching layer', 'how': ['Before rewriting, the agent answers four questions: what a top professional would do that you did not ask for, the rawest material they would start from, the specific methods and tools they would use, and what outside evidence would show the result is right.', 'Then it rewrites your prompt to ask for exactly that.', 'Choices that are yours to make are left open rather than decided for you.', 'Commit 88d4751 (#729, 19 Oct) restored this original prompt; its subject says the skill now returns only the rewrite.'], 'rules': ['I want a deeper version of this task, not a bigger one.', 'Name the rawest material they would start from instead of what has already been digested for me.', 'Leave choices that are mine to make open instead of deciding them.'], 'lesson': 'The four questions force specificity (raw sources, named methods, an external check) without growing scope, which is the usual failure of asking an agent to "improve" a prompt.'}]
